            <ArticleTitle>Frontal sinus osteoma associated with pneumocephalus.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>174-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The most common causes of intracranial air are head trauma and neurosurgical procedures. Less common etiologies include infection due to gas-forming organisms, mucoceles, tumours, congenital neuroenteric cysts, and dural defects. Here, we present a case of a frontal sinus osteoma associated with longstanding pneumocephalus.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
